A||||2||recluse||sway bar question (need suspention experts)||||5||
Z||000000||recluse||10-27-2001||10:48 PM||||I put a rear sway bar on my 87 GT (7/8)and noticed a pretty good difference in handling.  I noticed the back end sticks to the road a little better during high speed curves, and that its easier to get back under control when it does go out.  All cars i know of have a larger front bar compaired to the rear, or no rear bar at all.  Is this because of the extra weight up front due to the engine?  If so then shouldn't the rear bar be larger on the Fiero because of the rear engine?  I want to add a larger front sway bar on my Fiero but if its just going to make it more like it was originally then i might not want to.  Does this make sence to anyone?  Iv'e been wondering about it for a while.  It seems like the rear should have the 1 inch bar and the front have the 7/8 inch bar due to the weight being on the opposite end of the car comparied to front engine cars.  any help would be appreciated!||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||10-27-2001||10:55 PM||||The reason you want a larger bar on the front, is so that your front suspension can transmit more energy between them, this causes understeer.  All cars from the factory are made to have understeer, becuase it is much safer for most of the public.  If you have a larger bar on rear, your car will be much more prone to oversteer; the back end stepping out.  Most people don't know how to control this situation, so that's why most cars come standard with just a front anti sway bar but no or a smaller rear.  I have a stock front anti sway bar on the rear of my 87 coupe, and I dont have a problem with oversteer.<P>Weight really dosnet make a difference with sway bars, they don't judge the size by that.  Springs and shocks are judged by weight.  Your front springs are softer than your rears, becuase of the added weight in the rear.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||10-28-2001||11:35 AM||||Yeah,what he said,and the fact that the Fiero has a more weight in the rear makes it ultimately want to oversteer;if you can use stiffer rear springs/bar to get the oversteer sooner,than it's going to be easier to control for performance driving(possibly scary to the average driver).<BR>Wider rear tires help out the overall balance too,but not totally necessary,since the tire pressures can be adjusted to help also.||||reg||1
